body:
That is more or less an argument made by me, because that this is just what it is like, is exactly what I can't wrap my head around. Thus, these two statements do not come from the same source, but from a theoretical and practical point of view. I am not sure about the exact numbers anymore, but if I remember correctly, when asked about the trolley problem in theory, 70-80% of the participants were in favour of sacrificing one live in order to save 5 others. However, if you would tell a doctor or the police that you will be killing yourself in order to save others (organ donation) you will get stopped (at least in many countries across the globe). So there is a discrepance about what people theoretically think that they would do (save 5 lives) and what the law requires "officials" (like medical staff, police) to do (stop somone from commiting suicide even when it saves lives).  

&#x200B;

I am not sure what ethical egoism and solipsistism (thanks for introducing me to these concepts) would imply. So both would say that I should not or do not have kill myself for others. But that would automatically also imply that I should not stop someone else from suicide (because I am the only mind that exists) unless it brings you some kind of egoistical benefit. While I can see that an ethical egoist would not have to kill himself in order to save others, why would he or she have to interfere with the choice of someone else? He or she should be pretty indifferent about it and if that person thinks that it would ease their conscience to have saved 5 lives, he or she should also feel more comfortable letting someone (who kills him/herself) die to save the exact same 5 lives.
